      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to use the definitions of the geometric mean and harmonic mean of two positive real numbers. Let the two numbers be \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n\nThe geometric mean of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{ab} = \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nSquaring both sides, we get:\n\\[\nab = 3\n\\]\n\nThe harmonic mean of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\frac{2ab}{a + b} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(ab = 3\\) into the harmonic mean formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{a + b} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nSimplifying the left side, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{6}{a + b} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nCross-multiplying gives:\n\\[\n6 \\cdot 2 = 3 \\cdot (a + b)\n\\]\n\\[\n12 = 3(a + b)\n\\]\nDividing both sides by 3, we get:\n\\[\na + b = 4\n\\]\n\nNow we have a system of equations:\n1. \\(ab = 3\\)\n2. \\(a + b = 4\\)\n\nWe can solve this system by treating \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) as the roots of the quadratic equation \\(x^2 - (a+b)x + ab = 0\\). Substituting the values we have:\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x + 3 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -4\\), and \\(c = 3\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 - 12}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{4}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 \\pm 2}{2}\n\\]\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 + 2}{2} = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\frac{4 - 2}{2} = 1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the two numbers are \\(1\\) and \\(3\\). The final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1, 3}\n\\]",
